::Q1::[markdown]What happens when an existing file is opened in write mode (`w`)?{
~Its existing content is preserved and new content is added to the end
=Its existing content is truncated before writing
~The file is opened only for reading
~An error is always raised
}

::Q2::Which file mode allows you to append new content while preserving the existing contents of the file?{
~r+
~w
~w+
=a
}

::Q3::[markdown]Which statement correctly describes `w+` mode?{
~It allows only writing and always preserves existing content
~It allows appending and reading without changing existing content
~It allows only reading from an existing file
=It allows reading and writing, and an existing file is truncated when opened
}

::Q4::[markdown]In write mode, what happens when `seek(0)` is used before another `write()` operation?{
~It moves the cursor to the end of the file
~It changes the file mode from `w` to `a`
=It moves the cursor to the beginning of the file, so the third write overwrites existing data
~It deletes all existing content
}

::Q5::[markdown]Which statement correctly compares write mode (`w`) and append mode (`a`)?{
=`w` truncates an existing file, while `a` preserves its content and positions the cursor at the end
~`w` and `a` both prevent a new file from being created
~`w` preserves existing content, while `a` truncates it
~Both modes preserve existing content and write only at the end
}
